  ("{1}" 0.4582 0.5422 0.1581 0.1922 0.1922 0.0341 nil 0.1379 0.0840)
  ("open the door" 0.3500 0.6492 0.2235 0.2576 0.2576 0.0341 0.0138 0.2374 0.1073)
  ("W" 0.1176 0.1909 0.3136 0.3699 0.3699 0.0563 nil nil 0.0733)
  ("hat are the odds you’d open your refrigerator door and find a zombie" 0.1941 0.8657 0.3229 0.3394 0.3394 0.0165 0.0060 0.2714 0.0289)
  ("in there, knitting socks? The odds are about the same that a touchy-" 0.1941 0.8384 0.3447 0.3612 0.3612 0.0165 0.0060 0.3015 0.0188)
  ("feely, language-oriented person like me would end up as a professor of" 0.1176 0.8067 0.3665 0.3830 0.3830 0.0165 0.0059 0.2752 0.0529)
  ("engineering." 0.1176 0.2400 0.3883 0.4048 0.4048 0.0165 nil 0.1835 0.1224)
  ("Growing up, I hated math and science. I flunked my way through high" 0.1576 0.8318 0.4101 0.4267 0.4267 0.0165 0.0059 0.3349 0.0871)
  ("school math and science courses, and only started studying trigonometry—" 0.1176 0.8435 0.4319 0.4485 0.4485 0.0165 0.0059 0.2899 0.0642)
  ("remedial trigonometry—when I was twenty-six years old." 0.1176 0.6741 0.4538 0.4703 0.4703 0.0165 0.0061 0.3624 0.0854)
  ("As a youngster, even the simple concept of reading a clock face didn’t" 0.1576 0.8336 0.4756 0.4921 0.4921 0.0165 0.0060 0.2840 0.0256)
  ("seem to make sense to me. Why should the little hand point toward the hour?" 0.1176 0.8540 0.4974 0.5139 0.5139 0.0165 0.0060 0.3623 0.0489)
  ("Shouldn’t it be the big hand, since the hour was more important than the" 0.1176 0.8093 0.5192 0.5357 0.5357 0.0165 0.0060 0.3263 0.0948)
  ("minute? Did the clock read ten ten? Or one fifty? I was perpetually confused." 0.1176 0.8553 0.5410 0.5576 0.5576 0.0165 0.0060 0.3100 0.0763)
  ("Worse than my problems with clocks was the television. In those days before" 0.1176 0.8563 0.5629 0.5794 0.5794 0.0165 0.0060 0.3368 0.0622)
  ("the remote control, I didn’t even know which button turned the television on. I" 0.1176 0.8667 0.5847 0.6012 0.6012 0.0165 0.0059 0.3299 0.0281)
  ("watched a show only in the company of my brother or sister. They not only" 0.1176 0.8424 0.6065 0.6230 0.6230 0.0165 0.0059 0.3327 0.0776)
  ("could turn the TV on, but could also tune the channel to the program we wanted" 0.1176 0.8824 0.6283 0.6448 0.6448 0.0165 0.0060 0.3213 0.0541)
  ("to watch. Nice." 0.1176 0.2588 0.6501 0.6667 0.6667 0.0165 0.0071 0.3772 0.0176)
  ("All I could conclude, looking at my technical ineptitude and flunking" 0.1576 0.8200 0.6719 0.6885 0.6885 0.0165 0.0059 0.3037 0.0301)
  ("grades in math and science, was that I wasn’t very smart. At least, not that way. I" 0.1176 0.8820 0.6938 0.7103 0.7103 0.0165 0.0059 0.3612 0.0644)
  ("didn’t realize it then, but my self-portrait as being technically, scientifically," 0.1176 0.8471 0.7156 0.7321 0.7321 0.0165 0.0059 0.2845 0.0571)
  ("and mathematically incapable was shaping my life. At the root of it all was my" 0.1176 0.8706 0.7374 0.7539 0.7539 0.0165 0.0060 0.3657 0.0341)
  ("problem with mathematics. I had come to think of numbers and equations as" 0.1176 0.8480 0.7592 0.7757 0.7757 0.0165 0.0060 0.3514 0.0818)
  ("akin to one of life’s deadly diseases—to be avoided at all costs. I didn’t realize" 0.1176 0.8728 0.7810 0.7976 0.7976 0.0165 0.0060 0.3144 0.0412)
  ("then that there were simple mental tricks that could have brought math into" 0.1176 0.8341 0.8029 0.8194 0.8194 0.0165 0.0060 0.3413 0.0400)
  ("focus for me, tricks that are helpful not only for people who are bad at math," 0.1176 0.8565 0.8247 0.8412 0.8412 0.0165 0.0060 0.3148 0.0527)
  ("but also for those who are already good at it. I didn’t understand that my type" 0.1176 0.8575 0.8465 0.8630 0.8630 0.0165 0.0059 0.3128 0.0301)
  ("of thinking is typical of people who believe they can’t do math and science." 0.1176 0.8424 0.8683 0.8848 0.8848 0.0165 0.0061 0.2871 0.0208)
  ("Now, I realize that my problem was rooted in two distinctly different modes" 0.1176 0.8480 0.8901 0.9067 0.9067 0.0165 0.0059 0.3531 0.0506))))
